I don't have a specific product's tier sheet in this conversation, so here's a representative three-tier SaaS structure with placeholder numbers — replace them with your real figures and the logic below still holds.

:::intelligent-ui{v="1"}
root = Comparison("Tier comparison (illustrative)", [t1, t2, t3])
t1 = {name:"Basic", attributes:["$9 /user/mo, billed yearly","1 workspace, 3 projects","5 GB storage","Community support","No SSO"]}
t2 = {name:"Pro", attributes:["$29 /user/mo","Unlimited projects","100 GB storage","Email support, 24h response","Basic SSO"]}
t3 = {name:"Enterprise", attributes:["Custom (~$79 /user/mo)","Unlimited + audit logs","1 TB+ storage","Dedicated CSM, 1h SLA","SAML SSO + SCIM provisioning"]}
:::

**Best value: Pro**, on the usual reading of this shape of pricing.

The reasoning: Basic → Pro costs $20/user/mo and buys the two things that actually block teams (unlimited projects, 20× storage) plus support with a response commitment. Pro → Enterprise costs roughly 2.7× more and what you're really paying for is compliance and control — SSO with provisioning, audit logs, an SLA, a named contact. That's a cost of doing business for regulated or 50+ seat orgs, not extra product value for everyone else. So Pro wins unless you have a security review or a contractual uptime requirement.

